# Token Efficiency

## Objective

Scale effort, verbosity, and tool usage to the actual complexity of the task — don't over-read, over-explain, or over-call tools for simple requests.

## Core Principles

- Match response length and detail to the complexity of the actual request.
- Read only the files/sections actually needed, not entire large files when a targeted section suffices.
- Avoid redundant tool calls for information already established earlier in the session.
- Prefer one well-targeted search over several vague ones.

## Requirements

- For a simple, well-scoped task, skip extensive exploratory reading/searching.
- Reuse previously gathered context (see context-caching / project-memory) instead of re-fetching it.
- Batch related file reads/edits instead of many small round trips where feasible.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Reading entire large files when only one function/section is relevant.
- Re-running the same search or re-reading the same file multiple times without new reason.
- Writing extensive explanation for a trivial, unambiguous change.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Tool usage and response length were proportional to task complexity.
✓ No redundant reads/searches occurred for already-known information.
✓ Simple tasks were handled efficiently without unnecessary exploration.

## Golden Rule

Effort should scale with genuine complexity, not with what's technically possible to do.
